An analysis of the compilation failure shows that `fflib_SObjects` (which represents the newer, plain collection-based domain layer in fflib) does not extend `fflib_SObjectDomain`. Instead, both `fflib_SObjectDomain` and `fflib_SObjects` implement the core interfaces.

Because `Accounts` extends `fflib_SObjects`, it cannot be returned from a method declared to return `fflib_SObjectDomain`. To support these modern domain collections, fflib-apex-common uses `fflib_SObjectDomain.IConstructable2` (or simply implements the domain constructable interface that matches `fflib_SObjects` factories). 

For newer domain classes extending `fflib_SObjects` within the modern `fflib_Application.DomainFactory`, the inner constructor class should implement `fflib_SObjects.IConstructable` (or `fflib_IDomainConstructor`) rather than `fflib_SObjectDomain.IConstructable`. This matches the factory signature in newer versions where domain classes are plain collection classes.

