The issue occurs because passing `false` to `newQueryFactory(false)` actually specifies whether to include selector fields (`includeSelectorFields`), causing the query factory to omit the standard field list returned by `getSObjectFieldList()`. This resulted in the missing fields like `Opportunity.Name` and `Opportunity.StageName`.

To fix this, we must build the query factory using the standard `newQueryFactory()` method (which includes the selector fields), and then explicitly clear the default sort order from the query factory's orderings list before applying our custom sort. 

Here is the corrected and complete class:

File: force-app/main/default/classes/OpportunitiesSelector.cls
```apex
public inherited sharing class OpportunitiesSelector extends fflib_SObjectSelector {
    public Schema.SObjectType getSObjectType() {
        return Opportunity.SObjectType;
    }

    public List<Schema.SObjectField> getSObjectFieldList() {
        return new List<Schema.SObjectField>{
            Opportunity.Id,
            Opportunity.Name,
            Opportunity.AccountId,
            Opportunity.StageName,
            Opportunity.Amount,
            Opportunity.CloseDate
        };
    }

    public List<Opportunity> selectById(Set<Id> opportunityIds) {
        return (List<Opportunity>) selectSObjectsById(opportunityIds);
    }

    public List<Opportunity> selectTopOpenByAccountIds(Set<Id> accountIds, Integer maxRecords) {
        if (accountIds == null || accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return new List<Opportunity>();
        }

        // Create the query factory with the default selector fields included
        fflib_QueryFactory qf = newQueryFactory();

        // Clear the default selector sort orderings
        qf.getOrderings().clear();

        // Add parent Account Name
        qf.selectField('Account.Name');

        // Filter for open opportunities belonging to the given Account IDs
        qf.setCondition('AccountId IN :accountIds AND IsClosed = false');

        // Order by: highest Amount first, nulls last, then earliest CloseDate first
        qf.addOrdering('Amount', fflib_QueryFactory.SortOrder.DESCENDING, true);
        qf.addOrdering('CloseDate', fflib_QueryFactory.SortOrder.ASCENDING);

        // Limit the number of records returned
        qf.setLimit(maxRecords);

        return (List<Opportunity>) Database.query(qf.toSOQL());
    }
}
```

To enforce user mode in the queries generated by the fflib selector, we must explicitly enable User Mode data access on the Selector and ensure that any generated query includes the user mode security enforcement. 

In `fflib_SObjectSelector`, when we pass `true` to `includeDataAccessSFDCOrgDefaults` (the first parameter of the constructor), fflib configures the query factory to enforce security. However, to guarantee that the actual SOQL query is run in `USER_MODE` (i.e., contains `WITH USER_MODE`), we must configure the selector's data access settings correctly. 

Additionally, we must ensure that the `Credit_Review` field set is properly retrieved and its fields are dynamically added to the list of selected fields.

Here is the corrected and complete `AccountsSelector` class:

File: force-app/main/default/classes/AccountsSelector.cls
```apex
public inherited sharing class AccountsSelector extends fflib_SObjectSelector {

    public AccountsSelector() {
        // Configure selector to enforce CRUD and FLS in USER_MODE
        super(
            true, // includeDataAccessSFDCOrgDefaults
            true, // enforceCRUD
            true, // enforceFLS
            false // sortSelectFields
        );
    }

    public Schema.SObjectType getSObjectType() {
        return Account.SObjectType;
    }

    public List<Schema.SObjectField> getSObjectFieldList() {
        Set<Schema.SObjectField> fields = new Set<Schema.SObjectField>{
            Account.Id,
            Account.Name,
            Account.Industry
        };

        // Add fields from the Credit_Review field set dynamically
        Schema.FieldSet creditReviewFieldSet = SObjectType.Account.FieldSets.Credit_Review;
        if (creditReviewFieldSet != null) {
            for (Schema.FieldSetMember fsm : creditReviewFieldSet.getFields()) {
                fields.add(fsm.getSObjectField());
            }
        }

        return new List<Schema.SObjectField>(fields);
    }

    public override fflib_QueryFactory newQueryFactory(Boolean assertCRUD, Boolean enforceFLS, Boolean includeGridFields) {
        // Force the queries built by this selector to run WITH USER_MODE
        fflib_QueryFactory qf = super.newQueryFactory(assertCRUD, enforceFLS, includeGridFields);
        qf.setEnforceDataAccessType(System.AccessType.READABLE);
        return qf;
    }

    public List<Account> selectById(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        return (List<Account>) selectSObjectsById(accountIds);
    }

    public List<Account> selectByIdWithContacts(Set<Id> accountIds) {
        fflib_QueryFactory accountQueryFactory = newQueryFactory();

        // Add the contact subquery using ContactsSelector
        new ContactsSelector().addQueryFactorySubselect(accountQueryFactory);

        // Set the condition for the Account query
        accountQueryFactory.setCondition('Id IN :accountIds');

        return (List<Account>) Database.query(accountQueryFactory.toSOQL());
    }
}
